            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Artistic Changes Under Akhenaten</strong><br>\nUnder Akhenaten's rule, Egyptian art underwent a dramatic transformation. It moved away from the idealized forms of earlier periods to a more realistic portrayal of the human figure, including the depiction of the royal family with elongated and exaggerated features. This new artistic style was as revolutionary as his religious policies, breaking with the rigid conventions of Egyptian art that had lasted for centuries.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Akhenaten's Family</strong><br>\nAkhenaten was married to Nefertiti, one of the most famous queens of ancient Egypt, who played a significant role in the Aten worship. They were known to have had six daughters, and there is also speculation about their relationship with Tutankhamun, Akhenaten's possible son or successor, who would return Egypt to traditional religious practices after Akhenaten's death.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Amarna Letters</strong><br>\nDuring Akhenaten's reign, correspondence between the Egyptian administration and its foreign counterparts was conducted through a series of tablets known as the Amarna Letters. These tablets provide insight into the diplomatic relationships of the time and reveal that despite his internal religious focus, Akhenaten maintained Egypt's international presence and dealt with matters of state and foreign policy.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
