          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Aftermath of Akhenaten's Reign</strong><br>\nAfter Akhenaten's death, his religious innovations did not survive long. His successors, beginning with Tutankhamun, reinstated the traditional Egyptian gods and gradually erased evidence of Akhenaten's monotheistic experiment. The temples to the Aten were dismantled, and the capital was moved back to Thebes, signifying a return to the old religious order.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Rediscovery of Amarna</strong><br>\nThe city of Akhetaten was abandoned shortly after Akhenaten's death, and its buildings were dismantled, with their stone reused in other construction. The site was lost for centuries until its rediscovery in the 19th century, when archaeologists began to uncover the story of Akhenaten and his unique capital. The rediscovery provided an unprecedented glimpse into the Amarna period's life and art.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Akhenaten's Legacy</strong><br>\nAkhenaten is often referred to as history's first monotheist and is recognized for his attempt to restructure Egyptian religion. His reign is marked as a significant departure from traditional Egyptian culture, leaving a legacy that continues to intrigue scholars and the public alike. Despite his efforts to promote the Aten's worship, it was his radical changes that eventually led to his legacy being suppressed by subsequent pharaohs seeking to restore Egypt's traditional values and practices.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
